questions.explain:
False. Only a prototype ends with a semicolon. In a definition, the header is followed directly by the body:
```
int twice(int n);      // prototype: semicolon, no body
int twice(int n)       // header of the definition: no semicolon
{
    return n * 2;
}
```
If you put a semicolon after the header of a definition, that line becomes a prototype, and the block in braces under it belongs to no function. The program does not compile.

